<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finnish Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Finnish Civil War was fought from January to May 1918, shortly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s independence, between the socialist Reds, strongest in the industrial south, and the government's Whites under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-gustaf-emil-mannerheim">Carl Gustaf Emil Mannerheim</a>. With the help of German troops, the Whites won. About 36,000 people died, many in executions and in prison camps after the fighting, leaving deep divisions in Finnish society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war.jpg" alt="Finnish Civil War" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tampereen_taistelun_aikana_tuhoutunutta_Tammelan_kaupunginosaa_(26696844330).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> had been a grand duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> since 1809. Its population grew from 860,000 in 1810 to 3,130,000 in 1917, industry and a labour movement developed, and tension rose over the autocratic rule of the tsar and the undemocratic system of estates inherited from Swedish rule. The main cause of the war was the crisis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-provisional-government">Russian Provisional Government</a> restored Finland's autonomy, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> both conservatives and socialists came to support independence, though they disagreed about who should lead the nation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The final escalation began in early January 1918, when each action by one side drew a counteraction from the other. The Red Guards, led by a section of the Social Democratic Party, held the south, including nearly all the major towns and industrial centres. The Whites, led by General Mannerheim and built around the Jägers trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and volunteer Civil Guards, held the agrarian centre and north. The Reds' general offensive in February failed.</p><p>Mannerheim chose to strike first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampere">Tampere</a>, the main industrial town of the south-west, which held nearly 15,000 heavily armed Red Guards. His assault began on 16 March, and the Whites besieged the town. After German troops landed in April, the Reds' leaders withdrew from Helsinki to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>. Thousands of Reds tried to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, but most were encircled, and those in the Lahti area surrendered on 1 and 2 May. The war ended on 15 May with the capture of Fort Ino. Both sides carried out executions, known as the White Terror and the Red Terror.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Whites and German troops captured around 80,000 Red prisoners, including 5,000 women and 1,500 children, and held them in camps such as Suomenlinna and Hämeenlinna. Most of the deaths in the war occurred outside the battlefields, in the camps and the terror. Treason courts examined 76,000 cases and convicted 68,000 Reds, and of 555 death sentences 113 were carried out. About 15,000 children were left orphaned, and many moderate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> came to think of themselves as citizens of two nations.</p><p>A plan for a German-led Finnish monarchy collapsed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s surrender in November 1918, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> became an independent republic, confirmed by the Constitution Act of 17 July 1919. The war has several names, among them Civil War, Class War, War of Independence and Red Rebellion, each tied to a political viewpoint.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Finnish Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Independence was immediately followed by the <b>Civil War of January to May 1918</b> between the socialist Reds, based in the industrial south, and the government’s Whites under Carl Gustaf Emil Mannerheim, who won with German help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Finnish Civil War</b> the Red Guards adapted it as the workers' marching song &quot;Vapaa Venäjä&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/farewell-of-slavianka">Farewell of Slavianka</a></li></ul></section>
